      "math_academy_id": "q-101739",
      "sequence_position": 9,
      "problem": "Find the interval where the average rate of change of $f(x)$ equals $0$ given the following table of values of $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $\\,-6\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ | $\\,-2\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ | $\\,6\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,-7\\,$ | $\\,-5\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,-2\\,$ | $\\,5\\,$ | $\\,-5\\,$ | $\\,-8\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "We have $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=0$ if and only if $Δf=0.$ So\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & Δf=0 \\\\  & f(b)-f(a)=0 \\\\  & f(b)=f(a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means we have to find an interval  $[a,b]$ such that $f(a)=f(b).$\n\nSince $f(-4)=f(4)=-5,$ we conclude that $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=0$ on the interval $[-4,4].$",
